+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool declares a JSON Schema that is handed verbatim to the providers, so the schema the model was given is also the contract its call has to meet. `ToolRegistry.execute` is the one place that can hold the model to it: the input is validated there, before the executor runs, and a rejected call goes back to the model as a tool result it can read and correct — never as a thrown error, which would leave the conversation through the orchestrator's tool-call `Promise.all` and reach the client as a generic transport error.
+
+## Validation contract
+
+- `execute` validates `input` against the resolved tool definition's `inputSchema` before calling the executor; on failure it returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` describes the failure, naming a missing required property as required, and the executor is never invoked ([validated by: returns a rejection naming location as required and never calls the executor when required input is missing](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L15)).
+- Valid input reaches the executor unchanged and exactly once: a property present in `input` but absent from the schema is passed through as it arrived, with nothing coerced, defaulted, added or stripped ([validated by: passes valid input with an unlisted extra property through to the executor unchanged, exactly once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L39)).
+- A field of the wrong type is rejected with a message naming the field path and the expected type, and the message does not contain the offending value ([validated by: rejects a number where a string is required, naming the field path and expected type but never the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L59)).
+- Validation enforces `required`, declared types and `enum` only: it does not inject `additionalProperties: false`, coerce types, apply defaults, or offer an opt-out flag.
+- Tool input is where identifying data enters the engine, so a rejection names property paths and the constraint that failed and never a property value: neither the returned text nor any log line produced during the call contains an input value ([validated by: keeps an identifying input value out of the rejection text and every log line](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L79)).
+- `execute` still throws `Unknown tool: <name>` for an unregistered name, unchanged — an unknown tool name is a caller error, not model input a schema can correct ([validated by: still throws Unknown tool for an unregistered name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L159)).
+
+## Schema compilation
+
+- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time, so an uncompilable schema throws there with a message naming the tool and a broken schema fails at startup rather than mid-conversation ([validated by: throws at register time for an uncompilable schema, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L101)).
+- A static schema is compiled once, and every later `execute` call keeps validating against the schema compiled at register ([validated by: compiles a static schema once at register: ten execute calls keep validating against the register-time schema](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L136)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves per call, so each `execute` is validated against the schema the function returns at that call rather than a stale cached one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at that call, not a stale cached one](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L112)).
+
+## Orchestrator integration
+
+- A rejection is a tool result rather than an exception: `processMessage` resolves, the rejection text reaches the provider as the tool result of the failed call, and the corrected retry is bounded by the tool-round limit that stops a retry cycle from spinning ([validated by: returns a schema rejection to the model as a tool result it can read and correct on the next round](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L17)).
+
+## Out of scope
+
+Validating tool output, per-tool authorization, retry, timeout and rate limiting around tool execution, and returning an unknown tool name to the model instead of throwing are tracked separately and deliberately absent here.
